{"kind":"assert","text":"Rulings on ef3f150a for NF-4 (d2aff067). These came 25 minutes\nlate: I read your act at the 22:28 survey.\n\nQ5. Choose B. The options type gains a package-bytes port that\ntakes an address and a byte limit and returns the bytes or a typed\nfailure reason. MCP and the command line each build it from\n`@generalbusiness/dap-git/application`. The command line gets one\nnew allowance in scripts/active-source.test.mjs. The client gains\nno dependency on dap-git.\n\nQ6. Choose A. `declarationDigest`, its two helpers and the mark\ncatalogue built from `VERSIONS` move to the install module of Q7,\nand MCP imports them from there. One test fixes the digest of a\nfixed request to the value that main 7ea170c9 computes, so the\ncommand line and `dap mcp` store the same digest in one custody\ndirectory.\n\nQ7. Yes: a new subpath `@generalbusiness/dap-client/install`, in\nsrc/install-request.ts, on the layering list. The client root does\nnot import it. Keep it source-only, as `./node` is. The command\nline reaches it the way it reaches `./node` since NF-3. If the\npublic release of the command line cannot resolve a source-only\nsubpath, stop and tell me before you publish it.\n\nThe options type now carries four parts: the reader, the `admit`\nhook, the `send` port and the package-bytes port. Keep it at four.\nIf the work needs a fifth, ask first, because the function then\nowns too little.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}